ity—at a limit of integration or at some point between the limits of inte- gration. If the integrand ƒ is positive over the interval of integration, we can again interpret the improper integral as the area under the graph of ƒ and above the x-axis between the limits of integration."},{"x":0.36382,"y":0.84571,"w":0.59178,"h":0.03858,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Consider the region in the first quadrant that lies under the curve y = 1> 2x from x = 0 to x = 1 (Figure 8.12b). First we find the area of the portion from a to 1 (Figure 8.16):"},{"x":0.11803,"y":0.85022,"w":0.06476,"h":0.01024,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"1 a"},{"x":0.08775,"y":0.89166,"w":0.25636,"h":0.04297,"fs":0.01151,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 8.16 The area under this curve is an example of an improper integral of the second kind."},{"x":0.57712,"y":0.9007,"w":0.08663,"h":0.03407,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"2x = 22xd"},{"x":0.66621,"y":0.90077,"w":0.09998,"h":0.02316,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"1 = 2 - 22a."},{"x":0.57998,"y":0.90392,"w":0.01543,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"dx"}]}